<section class="w-full max-w-[640px] mx-auto px-[var(--section-x)] py-[var(--section-y)] border-t border-gray-200">
  <p class="font-sans text-sm text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest mb-8">If any of this sounds like you, text me.</p>

  <div class="space-y-10">
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-serif type-h3 mb-3 text-near-black">If you work somewhere and you want to be a better friend to your coworkers.</h3>
      <p class="font-sans text-base leading-relaxed text-near-black prose-body">
        <strong>You've thought about how to talk about Jesus with the people you see every day, but it always feels weird or forced.</strong> That's most of what I work on. No scripts, no awkward openers, no making it your whole personality. Just figuring out how to actually befriend people and be the one they come to with the honest questions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="font-serif type-h3 mb-3 text-near-black">If you run a business and you want it to reflect your faith.</h3>
      <p class="font-sans text-base leading-relaxed text-near-black prose-body">
        <strong>Some of it is quiet: how you treat your team, how you make decisions, how you handle a hard call.</strong> Some of it is loud. Both are valid. Let's talk about what fits your team.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="font-serif type-h3 mb-3 text-near-black">If you have hard questions and nowhere safe to ask them.</h3>
      <p class="font-sans text-base leading-relaxed text-near-black prose-body">
        <strong>About the Bible, suffering, science, the church, your own doubts.</strong> I'd rather hear the real questions than have you sit on them. I don't have every answer, but I've wrestled with most of the big ones and I know where the honest evidence is.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="font-serif type-h3 mb-3 text-near-black">If church feels flat and you're not sure why.</h3>
      <p class="font-sans text-base leading-relaxed text-near-black prose-body">
        <strong>You're not crazy and you're not alone.</strong> Church is a means to a mission, not the mission itself. If you're ready to stop watching from a pew and start living what we've actually been invited into, that's the conversation I love most.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
